    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d360">

                <pb facs="0612" n="1572" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1572"/>

                <head>No. 1073.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Straus.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-03-20"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 20,
                        1888</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 85.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 64, of the 27th
                    ultimo, relative to the eviction of the cavass at the consulate of Jerusalem
                    from his residence. You state that you informally brought the complaint to the
                    Grand Vizier, “who appeared to be of the opinion that such cavass was entitled
                    to the same privileges as to domicile as are accorded to an American citizen;
                    and he immediately communicated with the governor of Jerusalem, asking for
                    explanation for the course pursued.”</p>
                <p>Your reported action is entirely in the line of, and anticipating, the
                    Department’s instruction No. 84, of the 17th instant, which directed you to
                    ascertain the customary privilege in the premises, and is therefore
                    approved.</p>
                <p>Awaiting a further report upon the subject,</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
